// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
// release.bash's arguments and help (mise run release --help). mise rewraps the help to the
// terminal but keeps its line breaks: a paragraph, or a list's item, is one line.

long_about """
    Release the next CalVer version (vYYYY.MM.MICRO): run the Release workflow on origin's main, and follow it.

    The workflow, not this machine, makes the release. From the commit of main that it starts on, it:

    1. prepares the release (release:prepare): names the pending migrations with the version, and pins the deployment's image
    2. runs every check and every test on what it prepared, and builds the JAR
    3. builds and publishes the image
    4. commits the release to main as kludgeworks' GitHub App (kludgebot), signed by GitHub, and tags it (release:publish)
    5. publishes the GitHub release, with the JAR

    Nothing is committed or tagged until the JAR and the image are built. A run that fails leaves main and the tags as they were, and the next run makes the same version.

    If nothing changed since the latest release, the workflow makes no release.
    """

after_long_help "Main here must be origin's main, so that the release is of what you see. A release's version is in its migrations' names, which databases record, and in what the workflow published, so a tag is never moved: a bad release is fixed by the next one."

flag "-n --dry-run" env="RELEASE_DRY_RUN" help="Show the next version, the renames, the pin and the notes, without starting the workflow"
flag "--watch" negate="--no-watch" env="RELEASE_WATCH" default=#true help="Follow the run until it ends, and fail if it fails"

example "mise run release" header="Release" help="Starts the Release workflow on main and follows it"
example "mise run release --no-watch" header="Release, not followed" help="Starts the workflow and prints the link to its run"
example "mise run release --dry-run" header="See what would be released" help="The version, the renames, the pin and the notes"
